course: dictionary
lesson: 5818
title: Magnitude: how big or how strong
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Magnitude means how big or how strong something is. In «a magnitude 9 earthquake», the number tells you how strong the quake was. The words around it give clues: «sent a tsunami into Japan» and «killed about 18,000 people» show that a magnitude 9 quake is very powerful.
explain: Magnitude can also mean how important or serious something is. In «a problem of great magnitude», great tells you the problem is very big in importance, not in a size you could measure with a ruler. Swap in a meaning to check: «a problem of great seriousness» still makes sense, so magnitude means seriousness here.
explain: In maths, magnitude is the size of a number without its sign, so the magnitude of -7 is 7. Scientists also use «order of magnitude», which means roughly how many tens or powers of ten a number is. When a word has several meanings, the context tells you which one to use.
fact: Magnitude means size, strength, or how important something is.
fact: «A magnitude 9.1 earthquake» is measured on a scale of strength.
fact: Each step up in earthquake magnitude means about 32 times more energy released.
fact: «The strongest earthquake ever recorded» was in Chile in 1960, magnitude about 9.5.
fact: In maths, the magnitude of a number is its size without the sign: the magnitude of -7 is 7.
exercise: «The 2004 tsunami followed a magnitude 9.1 earthquake off Sumatra.» In this sentence, what does magnitude 9.1 tell us? | The name of the island | The number of people who lived there | *The strength of the earthquake | The colour of the sea
exercise: What does «a problem of great magnitude» mean? | Happening at night | Funny and light-hearted | *Very serious and important | Small and easy to fix
exercise: Maths: what is the magnitude of -7? | 0 | 14 | -7 | *7
test: Each step up in earthquake magnitude means about how much more energy released? | About 10 times less | Exactly 2 times more | Exactly the same amount | *About 32 times more
test: «The strongest earthquake ever recorded was in Chile in 1960, magnitude about 9.5.» What was its magnitude, about? | *9.5 | 12.0 | 6.0 | 1.5
exam: Ben reads, «The magnitude of the problem was so great we needed help.» What does magnitude mean here? | The colour of the problem | How long the problem lasted | The number of people in the problem | *How big or serious the problem was
